.vite
# Developer-LOCAL env overrides. frontend/Dockerfile.prod does `COPY . .`, so
# anything not excluded here is copied into the image and read by vite at build
# time. In a production build vite reads `.env`, `.env.production`,
# `.env.local` and `.env.production.local`; of those only `.env.production` is
# tracked, so the other three are developer-local files that could set
# VITE_SHOW_DEMO and compile the seeded demo credentials into the production
# bundle.
# frontend/.env.production is deliberately NOT excluded: it is tracked, it
# holds only public values, and the build needs it.
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE SAME RULE, FOR THIS CONTEXT (added 2026-11-11).
#
# The rule the root .dockerignore already states -- nothing that .gitignore
# excludes may enter the image -- applies to THIS context as well, because
# this context is its own docker build context and its Dockerfile does
# `COPY . .`. The rules below are the root's rule set translated here.
#
# .env.production and .env.example are re-included: they are TRACKED, the
# web build reads them, and the published archive has to contain them.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.gitignore
**/.gitignore
Dockerfile
Dockerfile.*
**/Dockerfile*
docker-compose.yml
.env.*
*.env
**/*.env
**/.env
**/.env.*
!.env.example
